Yes, exactly! You can toggle a destination on/off just by changing `active` and running `terraform apply`. Here's a clean module pattern for it:

**Module structure:**
```hcl
# modules/newrelic-destination/main.tf
resource "newrelic_notification_destination" "this" {
  name   = var.name
  type   = var.type
  active = var.active    # <-- the toggle

  property {
    key   = var.property_key
    value = var.property_value
  }
}
```

```hcl
# modules/newrelic-destination/variables.tf
variable "name" {}
variable "type" {}
variable "property_key" {}
variable "property_value" {}

variable "active" {
  type    = bool
  default = true    # enabled by default
}
```

**Calling the module:**
```hcl
module "my_destination" {
  source = "./modules/newrelic-destination"

  name           = "my-email-destination"
  type           = "EMAIL"
  property_key   = "email"
  property_value = "alerts@example.com"

  active = false   # just flip this true/false and re-apply
}
```

**The workflow is simple:**

- Want to **disable** it? Set `active = false` → `terraform apply`
- Want to **re-enable** it? Set `active = true` → `terraform apply`

Terraform will just do an **in-place update** (no destroy/recreate), so it's a lightweight change. You can even drive it from a `tfvars` file or CI/CD variable so you never have to touch the module code itself:

```hcl
# terraform.tfvars
destination_active = false
```

```hcl
# root main.tf
module "my_destination" {
  source = "./modules/newrelic-destination"
  ...
  active = var.destination_active
}
```

That way flipping an alert destination on or off in production is just a variable change + apply.
